(window.RW_DISPLAY = window.RW_DISPLAY || {})["test11_1B"] = [{"id":"c14daa3c","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Close analysis of the painting Girl with a Flute, long attributed to the seventeenth-century Dutch painter Johannes Vermeer, has revealed subtle deviations from the artist’s signature techniques. These variations suggest that the work may be that of a student under Vermeer’s tutelage — potentially ______ our understanding of Vermeer as a solitary artist.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Close analysis of the painting Girl with a Flute, long attributed to the seventeenth-century Dutch painter Johannes Vermeer, has revealed subtle","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"deviations from the artist’s signature techniques. These variations suggest that the work may be that of a student under Vermeer’s tutelage—","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"potentially ______ our understanding of Vermeer as a solitary artist.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["negating","prefiguring","entrenching","substantiating"]},{"id":"fb8b56bc","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"The following text is adapted from George Bernard Shaw’s 1912 play Pygmalion. Henry Higgins has just arrived at the house of his mother (Mrs. Higgins). She is expecting her friends to visit soon. MRS. HIGGINS: I’m serious, Henry. You offend all my friends: they stop coming whenever they meet you. HIGGINS: Nonsense! I know I have no small talk; but people don’t mind. MRS. HIGGINS: Oh! don’t they? Small talk indeed! What about your large talk? Really, dear, you mustn’t stay.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is adapted from George Bernard Shaw’s 1912 play Pygmalion. Henry Higgins has just arrived at the house of his mother (Mrs.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Higgins). She is expecting her friends to visit soon.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"MRS. HIGGINS: I’m serious, Henry. You offend all my friends: they stop coming whenever they meet you.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"HIGGINS: Nonsense! I know I have no small talk; but people don’t mind.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"MRS. HIGGINS: Oh! don’t they? Small talk indeed! What about your large talk? Really, dear, you mustn’t stay.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?","choices":["To describe what Henry’s mother does when she goes out with her friends","To show that Henry’s mother wants him to leave","To present a detailed account of what Henry’s home looks like","To explain why Henry often visits his mother"]},{"id":"c68ceeff","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 Today the starchy root cassava is found in many dishes across West Africa, but its rise to popularity was slow. Portuguese traders brought cassava from Brazil to the West African coast in the 1500s. But at this time, people living in the capitals further inland had little contact with coastal communities. Thus, cassava remained relatively unknown to most of the region’s inhabitants until the 1800s. Text 2 Cassava’s slow adoption into the diet of West Africans is mainly due to the nature of the crop itself. If not cooked properly, cassava can be toxic. Knowledge of how to properly prepare cassava needed to spread before the food could grow in popularity. The arrival of formerly enslaved people from Brazil in the 1800s, who brought their knowledge of cassava and its preparation with them, thus directly fueled the spread of this crop.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Today the starchy root cassava is found in many dishes across West Africa, but its rise to popularity was slow. Portuguese traders brought","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"cassava from Brazil to the West African coast in the 1500s. But at this time, people living in the capitals further inland had little contact with","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"coastal communities. Thus, cassava remained relatively unknown to most of the region’s inhabitants until the 1800s.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Cassava’s slow adoption into the diet of West Africans is mainly due to the nature of the crop itself. If not cooked properly, cassava can be toxic.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Knowledge of how to properly prepare cassava needed to spread before the food could grow in popularity. The arrival of formerly enslaved","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"people from Brazil in the 1800s, who brought their knowledge of cassava and its preparation with them, thus directly fueled the spread of this","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"crop.","indent":0}],"question":"Based on the texts, the author of Text 1 and the author of Text 2 would most likely agree with which statement?","choices":["Cassava did not become a significant crop in West Africa until long after it was first introduced.","Several of the most commonly grown crops in West Africa are originally from Brazil.","The climate of the West African coast in the 1500s prevented cassava’s spread in the region.","The most commonly used methods to cook cassava today date to the 1500s."]},{"id":"f6352bd3","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"Many archaeologists assume that large-scale engineering projects in ancient societies required an elite class to plan and direct the necessary labor. However, recent discoveries, such as the excavation of an ancient canal near the Gulf Coast of Alabama, have complicated this picture. Using radiocarbon dating, a team of researchers concluded that the 1.39-kilometer-long canal was most likely constructed between 576 and 650 CE by an Indigenous society that was relatively free of social classes.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Many archaeologists assume that large-scale engineering projects in ancient societies required an elite class to plan and direct the necessary","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"labor. However, recent discoveries, such as the excavation of an ancient canal near the Gulf Coast of Alabama, have complicated this picture.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Using radiocarbon dating, a team of researchers concluded that the 1.39-kilometer-long canal was most likely constructed between 576 and 650","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"CE by an Indigenous society that was relatively free of social classes.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It describes a common view among archaeologists, then discusses a recent finding that challenges that view.","It outlines a method used in some archaeological fieldwork, then explains why an alternative method is superior to it.","It presents contradictory conclusions drawn by archaeologists, then evaluates a study that has apparently resolved that contradiction.","It identifies a gap in scientific research, then presents a strategy used by some archaeologists to remedy that gap."]},{"id":"74ee7737","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Whether Carmen Lomas Garza is creating small paintings and illustrations or large public artworks —such as Baile, a copper cutout of traditional Mexican dance in the San Francisco International Airport — she is ______ direct experience, drawing from memories of her childhood in Texas or details of her current surroundings in California.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Whether Carmen Lomas Garza is creating small paintings and illustrations or large public artworks—such as Baile, a copper cutout of traditional","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Mexican dance in the San Francisco International Airport—she is ______ direct experience, drawing from memories of her childhood in Texas or","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"details of her current surroundings in California.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["complimented by","uncertain about","unbothered by","inspired by"]},{"id":"6286dd96","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"The following text is adapted from Frances Hodgson Burnett’s 1905 novel A Little Princess. Sara is a young student at a school in London. Sara not only could tell stories, but she adored telling them. When she sat or stood in the midst of a circle and began to invent wonderful things, her green eyes grew big and shining, her cheeks flushed, and, without knowing that she was doing it, she began to act and made what she told lovely or alarming by the raising or dropping of her voice. As used in the text,","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is adapted from Frances Hodgson Burnett’s 1905 novel A Little Princess. Sara is a young student at a school in London.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Sara not only could tell stories, but she adored telling them. When she sat or stood in the midst of a circle and began to invent","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"wonderful things, her green eyes grew big and shining, her cheeks flushed, and, without knowing that she was doing it, she began","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"to act and made what she told lovely or alarming by the raising or dropping of her voice.","indent":2}],"question":"As used in the text, what does the word “invent” most nearly mean?","choices":["Mislead","Disguise","Create","Discover"],"underline":"invent","underline_segments":["invent"]},{"id":"88bb0f6f","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 A team led by Bernardo Strassburg has found that rewilding farmland (returning the land to its natural state) could help preserve biodiversity and offset carbon emissions. The amount of farmland that would need to be restored, they found, is remarkably low. Rewilding a mere 15% of the world’s current farmland would prevent 60% of expected species extinctions and help absorb nearly 299 gigatons of carbon dioxide — a clear win in the fight against the biodiversity and climate crises. Text 2 While Strassburg’s team’s findings certainly offer encouraging insight into the potential benefits of rewilding, it’s important to consider potential effects on global food supplies. The researchers suggest that to compensate for the loss of food-producing land, remaining farmland would need to produce even more food. Thus, policies focused on rewilding farmland must also address strategies for higher-yield farming.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"A team led by Bernardo Strassburg has found that rewilding farmland (returning the land to its natural state) could help preserve biodiversity and","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"offset carbon emissions. The amount of farmland that would need to be restored, they found, is remarkably low. Rewilding a mere 15% of the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"world’s current farmland would prevent 60% of expected species extinctions and help absorb nearly 299 gigatons of carbon dioxide—a clear win","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"in the fight against the biodiversity and climate crises.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"While Strassburg’s team’s findings certainly offer encouraging insight into the potential benefits of rewilding, it’s important to consider potential","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"effects on global food supplies. The researchers suggest that to compensate for the loss of food-producing land, remaining farmland would","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"need to produce even more food. Thus, policies focused on rewilding farmland must also address strategies for higher-yield farming.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best describes a difference in how the author of Text 1 and the author of Text 2 view Strassburg’s team’s study?","choices":["The author of Text 2 approaches the study’s findings with some caution, whereas the author of Text 1 is optimistic about the reported potential environmental benefits.","The author of Text 2 claims that the percentage of farmland identified by Strassburg’s team is too low for rewilding to achieve meaningful results, whereas the author of Text 1 thinks the percentage is sufficient.","The author of Text 2 believes that the results described by Strassburg’s team are achievable in the near future, whereas the author of Text 1 argues that they likely aren’t.","The author of Text 2 focuses on rewilding’s effect on carbon emissions, whereas the author of Text 1 focuses on its effect on biodiversity."]},{"id":"6c086e70","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Businesses typically try to promote themselves, but research by Michael Luca, Abhishek Nagaraj, and Gauri Subramani suggests that some do not ______ their marketing. The researchers studied restaurants in Texas and found that those listed on a popular review website (which can be done at no cost) had revenue increases of 5 percent on average, but 33 percent of restaurants studied never listed on the site.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Businesses typically try to promote themselves, but research by Michael Luca, Abhishek Nagaraj, and Gauri Subramani suggests that some do","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"not ______ their marketing. The researchers studied restaurants in Texas and found that those listed on a popular review website (which can be","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"done at no cost) had revenue increases of 5 percent on average, but 33 percent of restaurants studied never listed on the site.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["adorn","optimize","endorse","counteract"]},{"id":"1c69ff20","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"For many years, the only existing fossil evidence of mixopterid eurypterids — an extinct family of large aquatic arthropods known as sea scorpions and related to modern arachnids and horseshoe crabs — came from four species living on the paleocontinent of Laurussia. In a discovery that expands our understanding of the geographical distribution of mixopterids, paleontologist Bo Wang and others have identified fossilized remains of a new mixopterid species, Terropterus xiushanensis, that lived over 400 million years ago on the paleocontinent of Gondwana.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"For many years, the only existing fossil evidence of mixopterid eurypterids—an extinct family of large aquatic arthropods known as sea scorpions","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"and related to modern arachnids and horseshoe crabs—came from four species living on the paleocontinent of Laurussia. In a discovery that","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"expands our understanding of the geographical distribution of mixopterids, paleontologist Bo Wang and others have identified fossilized remains","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"of a new mixopterid species, Terropterus xiushanensis, that lived over 400 million years ago on the paleocontinent of Gondwana.","indent":0}],"question":"According to the text, why was Wang and his team’s discovery of the Terropterus xiushanensis fossil significant?","choices":["The fossil constitutes the first evidence found by scientists that mixopterids lived more than 400 million years ago.","The fossil helps establish that mixopterids are more closely related to modern arachnids and horseshoe crabs than previously thought.","The fossil helps establish a more accurate timeline of the evolution of mixopterids on the paleocontinents of Laurussia and Gondwana.","The fossil constitutes the first evidence found by scientists that mixopterids existed outside the paleocontinent of Laurussia."]},{"id":"0021d326","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Olms are salamanders that live in underwater caves. Scientists once thought that olms stay in their caves all their lives. However, Raoul Manenti and team claim that olms regularly come to the surface to perform important activities such as finding food.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Olms are salamanders that live in underwater caves. Scientists once thought that olms stay in their caves all their lives. However, Raoul Manenti","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"and team claim that olms regularly come to the surface to perform important activities such as finding food.","indent":0}],"question":"Which finding, if true, would most strongly support the underlined claim?","choices":["Researchers determine that olms don’t breed often.","Researchers confirm that olms live in only a few cave systems.","Researchers discover that earthworms from surface soils are a major part of olms’ diet.","Researchers learn that olms’ brains differ from other salamanders’ brains."],"underline":"olms regularly come to the surface to perform important activities such as finding food.","underline_segments":["olms regularly come to the surface to perform important activities such as finding food."]},{"id":"30c3aa98","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Copper Production for Three States, 1889-1909 400 Yearly copper production (in millions of pounds) 300 200 100 0 1889 1902 1909 Year Montana Arizona Michigan Copper had been mined in the US for thousands of years, but large-scale commercial mining of copper took off starting in the late 1800s. This was due to several factors. Technological advancements in the mining industry led to improvements in the production of copper. This helped the country keep up with the growing number of people wanting to buy copper starting in the 1890s. At the same time, the growth of the railroad system made the transportation of copper in large batches much easier. Several states saw rapid growth in the production of this resource, for example: ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"chart","note":"[Graph: Copper Production for Three States, 1889–1909 — not available here]"},{"type":"text","text":"Copper had been mined in the US for thousands of years, but large-scale commercial mining of copper took off starting in the late 1800s. This was due to several factors. Technological advancements in the mining industry led to improvements in the production of copper. This helped the country keep up with the growing number of people wanting to buy copper starting in the 1890s. At the same time, the growth of the railroad system made the transportation of copper in large batches much easier. Several states saw rapid growth in the production of this resource, for example: ______"}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses the data in the graph to complete the example?","choices":["The rise in copper production in Michigan slowed from 1902 to 1909.","Montana and Arizona produced more copper than Michigan did in 1909.","Fewer than 100 million pounds of copper were produced in Arizona in 1889.","Copper production rose significantly from 1889 to 1909 for Arizona, Michigan, and Montana."]},{"id":"d52fca9f","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Many music streaming services also function as social media platforms: by giving users the option to follow friends and share curated playlists or information about their listening history, these platforms allow people to convey their music preferences and listening activities directly to their social networks. In a 2023 study, researcher Michael James Walsh interviewed frequent users of a popular music streaming platform to investigate how its social media dimensions shape their listening habits. Walsh found that these dimensions tended to make study participants feel more mindful of how their listening activities may be perceived, which in turn influenced how they managed those activities.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Many music streaming services also function as social media platforms: by giving users the option to follow friends and share curated playlists","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"or information about their listening history, these platforms allow people to convey their music preferences and listening activities directly to","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"their social networks. In a 2023 study, researcher Michael James Walsh interviewed frequent users of a popular music streaming platform to","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"investigate how its social media dimensions shape their listening habits. Walsh found that these dimensions tended to make study participants","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"feel more mindful of how their listening activities may be perceived, which in turn influenced how they managed those activities.","indent":0}],"question":"Which quotation from a music streaming platform user would best illustrate Walsh’s finding?","choices":["“When I create playlists or choose songs to listen to, I often pick songs that I think the friends who are following me might be interested in listening to, rather than what I might actually prefer listening to in that moment.”","“Because the predictions the platform makes about what new songs I might like depend on my past listening habits, at times its recommendations can feel overly repetitive and unvarying.”","“The social aspects of music streaming make it really easy to create a sense of a shared listening experience with my friends, even if we aren’t in the same room.”","“Listening to music through a streaming platform feels a lot more passive, since I don’t need to make any deliberate choices about what music I’m going to consume, like I do when buying digital downloads or records and CDs.”"]},{"id":"e37f79a7","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Housing Starts in the US, January –April 2022 (in thousands) Month Housing starts January 1,669 February 1,771 March 1,713 April 1,803 When construction of a single-family house begins, it is called a housing start. In the first four months of 2022, the highest number of housing starts in the United States was in ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"table","title":"Housing Starts in the US, January–April 2022 (in thousands)","headers":["Month","Housing starts"],"rows":[["January","1,669"],["February","1,771"],["March","1,713"],["April","1,803"]]},{"type":"text","text":"When construction of a single-family house begins, it is called a housing start. In the first four months of 2022, the highest number of housing starts in the United States was in ______"}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?","choices":["April.","March.","January.","February."]},{"id":"d1b8a9ad","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"Disco remains one of the most ridiculed popular music genres of the late twentieth century. But as scholars have argued, the genre is far less superficial than many people believe. Take the case of disco icon Donna Summer: she may have been associated with popular songs about love and heartbreak (subjects hardly unique to disco, by the way), but like many Black women singers before her, much of her music also reflects concerns about community and identity. These concerns are present in many of the genre’s greatest songs, and they generally don’t require much digging to reveal.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Disco remains one of the most ridiculed popular music genres of the late twentieth century. But as scholars have argued, the genre is far less","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"superficial than many people believe. Take the case of disco icon Donna Summer: she may have been associated with popular songs about love","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"and heartbreak (subjects hardly unique to disco, by the way), but like many Black women singers before her, much of her music also reflects","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"concerns about community and identity. These concerns are present in many of the genre’s greatest songs, and they generally don’t require","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"much digging to reveal.","indent":0}],"question":"What does the text most strongly suggest about the disco genre?","choices":["It has been unjustly ignored by most scholars despite the importance of the themes addressed by many of the genre’s songs.","It evolved over time from a superficial genre focused on romance to a genre focused on more serious concerns.","It has been unfairly dismissed for the inclusion of subject matter that is also found in other musical genres.","It gave rise to a Black women’s musical tradition that has endured even though the genre itself faded in the late twentieth century."]},{"id":"bcf2f169","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Inferences","passage":"Ana Castillo’s 1986 novel The Mixquiahuala Letters is a story told entirely through expressive letters from the narrator to her friend —letters that Castillo suggests could be read in several different orders. As they began reading it in class, some students remarked that they found the novel’s letter format daunting and its treatment of gender relations old-fashioned. The professor, however, pointed out that the novel is written in modern-sounding language and addresses issues that still matter today, suggesting that The Mixquiahuala Letters ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Ana Castillo’s 1986 novel The Mixquiahuala Letters is a story told entirely through expressive letters from the narrator to her friend—letters that","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Castillo suggests could be read in several different orders. As they began reading it in class, some students remarked that they found the novel’s","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"letter format daunting and its treatment of gender relations old-fashioned. The professor, however, pointed out that the novel is written in","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"modern-sounding language and addresses issues that still matter today, suggesting that The Mixquiahuala Letters______","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["has more to say about gender relations than other novels from the same period.","is more relevant to contemporary audiences than it may seem at first.","is easier to read than many contemporary novels that focus on friendship.","is best understood after multiple readings in different orders."]},{"id":"aa443c4b","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"Recent pollen analyses of the Aran Islands have led some researchers to propose that the now treeless islands were once wooded. This hypothesis ______ that certain trees, such as P. sylvestris, survived without interruption or human intervention throughout the Holocene cannot stand, researchers Michael O Connell and Karen Molloy counter, unless other explanations can first be ruled out.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Recent pollen analyses of the Aran Islands have led some researchers to propose that the now treeless islands were once wooded. This","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"hypothesis ______ that certain trees, such as P. sylvestris, survived without interruption or human intervention throughout the Holocene cannot","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"stand, researchers Michael O’Connell and Karen Molloy counter, unless other explanations can first be ruled out.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["suggesting","suggested","suggests","has suggested"]},{"id":"da53d726","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"In February 1919, following the end of the First World War, women from ten countries around the world convened the Inter-Allied Women’s Conference in Paris. The conference’s goals were ______ ensure women’s participation in the proceedings of the Paris Peace Conference, to secure the right of women to serve in the League of Nations, and to advocate for human rights.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In February 1919, following the end of the First World War, women from ten countries around the world convened the Inter-Allied Women’s","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Conference in Paris. The conference’s goals were ______ ensure women’s participation in the proceedings of the Paris Peace Conference, to","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"secure the right of women to serve in the League of Nations, and to advocate for human rights.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["threefold: to","threefold. To","threefold to","threefold; to"]},{"id":"145d5ca7","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"Gathering accurate data on water flow in the United States is challenging because of the country’s millions of miles of ______ the volume and speed of water at any given location can vary drastically over time.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Gathering accurate data on water flow in the United States is challenging because of the country’s millions of miles of ______ the volume and","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"speed of water at any given location can vary drastically over time.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["waterways and the fact that,","waterways, and the fact that,","waterways, and, the fact that","waterways and the fact that"]},{"id":"035fd57e","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"The rough frog is a species of amphibian native to Australia. Currently, the frog’s range ______ parts of northern New South Wales and southeastern Queensland.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The rough frog is a species of amphibian native to Australia. Currently, the frog’s range ______ parts of northern New South Wales and","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"southeastern Queensland.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["includes","included","will have included","had included"]},{"id":"4a90a978","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"In 1990, California native and researcher Ellen Ochoa left her position as chief of the Intelligent Systems Technology Branch at a NASA research center ______ the space agency’s astronaut training program.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In 1990, California native and researcher Ellen Ochoa left her position as chief of the Intelligent Systems Technology Branch at a NASA research","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"center ______ the space agency’s astronaut training program.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["to join","is joining","joined","joins"]},{"id":"96499989","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"áe’s song lyrics, including her recurring nods to the Fans of science fiction will ______ multiple references to classic sci-fi stories in Janelle Mon plot of the 1927 sci-fi film Metropolis .","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Fans of science fiction will ______ multiple references to classic sci-fi stories in Janelle Monáe’s song lyrics, including her recurring nods to the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"plot of the 1927 sci-fi film Metropolis.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["appreciate the","appreciate. The","appreciate, the","appreciate: the"]},{"id":"f5959727","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"Modernista architects championed nature in their designs. ______ the wavy staircase and ornate floral tilework of Hospital de Sant Pau, a í Modernista hospital designed by Llu’s Dom ènech i Montaner, couldn’t exactly grow in a forest. Still, one sees natural influences in Domènech i Montaner’s penchant for curves (rather than right angles) and plant- and animal-inspired flourishes.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Modernista architects championed nature in their designs. ______ the wavy staircase and ornate floral tilework of Hospital de Sant Pau, a","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Modernista hospital designed by Lluís Domènech i Montaner, couldn’t exactly grow in a forest. Still, one sees natural influences in Domènech i","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Montaner’s penchant for curves (rather than right angles) and plant- and animal-inspired flourishes.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Furthermore,","Similarly,","Of course,","Thus,"]},{"id":"9f1a0d91","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"“Tulip mania”— the rapid rise and sudden fall of the price of tulip bulbs in seventeenth-century Amsterdam — is often cited as an example of the perils of rampant market speculation. However, recent research has demonstrated that the episode was neither as frenzied nor as disastrous as has been thought. The popular myth surrounding it, ______ should be regarded with some skepticism.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"“Tulip mania”—the rapid rise and sudden fall of the price of tulip bulbs in seventeenth-century Amsterdam—is often cited as an example of the","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"perils of rampant market speculation. However, recent research has demonstrated that the episode was neither as frenzied nor as disastrous as","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"has been thought. The popular myth surrounding it, ______ should be regarded with some skepticism.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["for example,","by contrast,","nevertheless,","therefore,"]},{"id":"0c13dea9","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"The chemical trimethylamine N-oxide not only gives fish their fishy smell but also protects them from crushing hydrostatic pressure in deep waters. Trimethylamine N-oxide strengthens the bonds between water molecules in a fish’s body. ______ these water molecules maintain their linked structure at extreme depths, thus preventing pressure-related damage.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The chemical trimethylamine N-oxide not only gives fish their fishy smell but also protects them from crushing hydrostatic pressure in deep","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"waters. Trimethylamine N-oxide strengthens the bonds between water molecules in a fish’s body. ______ these water molecules maintain their","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"linked structure at extreme depths, thus preventing pressure-related damage.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Nevertheless,","As a result,","However,","For instance,"]},{"id":"85c0c0f0","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Texture analysis and historical analysis are two approaches to art criticism. Texture analysis examines how surfaces are visually represented in an artwork. Such an analysis of Giorgione’s Youth Holding an Arrow might consider how the painting’s blended colors make the subject’s skin appear smooth in texture. Historical analysis considers the historical context in which a work was created. Such an analysis of Diego Velázquez’s Las Meninas might consider how the painting’s depiction of the artist with King Philip IV symbolizes art’s historical ties to power. The student wants to present historical analysis to an audience unfamiliar with the concept.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Texture analysis and historical analysis are two approaches to art criticism.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Texture analysis examines how surfaces are visually represented in an artwork.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Such an analysis of Giorgione’s Youth Holding an Arrow might consider how the painting’s blended colors make the subject’s skin appear","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"smooth in texture.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Historical analysis considers the historical context in which a work was created.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Such an analysis of Diego Velázquez’s Las Meninas might consider how the painting’s depiction of the artist with King Philip IV","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"symbolizes art’s historical ties to power.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["A texture analysis of Youth Holding an Arrow might consider how the painting’s blended colors make the subject’s skin appear smooth in texture.","Texture analysis differs from historical analysis in that texture analysis examines how surfaces are visually represented in an artwork.","An approach to art criticism, historical analysis considers the historical context in which a work was created.","Las Meninas’s depiction of the artist with King Philip IV symbolizes art’s historical ties to power."]},{"id":"570dd854","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Here I Have Returned is a sculpture by Egyptian American artist Sherin Guirguis. It is a large, curved strip of wood inspired by the shape of a sistrum. A sistrum is a curved musical instrument played by ancient Egyptian priestesses in ceremonies. Guirguis says that the sculpture symbolizes “women who have lifted and supported Egyptian society and culture.” Overall, Guirguis wants her works to “engage audiences in a dialogue about power, agency, and social transformation.” The student wants to use a quotation from Guirguis to explain what the sculpture represents.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Here I Have Returned is a sculpture by Egyptian American artist Sherin Guirguis.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"It is a large, curved strip of wood inspired by the shape of a sistrum.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"A sistrum is a curved musical instrument played by ancient Egyptian priestesses in ceremonies.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Guirguis says that the sculpture symbolizes “women who have lifted and supported Egyptian society and culture.”","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Overall, Guirguis wants her works to “engage audiences in a dialogue about power, agency, and social transformation.”","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Guirguis, whose works include a sculpture that is a large, curved strip of wood, has explained that she wants her work to create a dialogue with audiences.","Inspired by the sistrum played by Egyptian priestesses, Here I Have Returned symbolizes “women who have lifted and supported Egyptian society and culture,” according to Guirguis.","According to Guirguis, the curved strip of wood used in Here I Have Returned was inspired by the sistrum, a musical instrument played by ancient Egyptian priestesses in ceremonies.","Guirguis, the sculptor of Here I Have Returned, wants her works to “engage audiences in a dialogue about power, agency, and social transformation.”"]},{"id":"fdd9a360","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: The popular wood-wide web theory posits that trees can communicate and exchange resources with one another via common mycorrhizal networks (CMNs) of fungi. Ecologist Dr. Suzanne Simard first suggested this theory in 1997. She described trees as “super-cooperators.” In the 2022 study “The Decay of the Wood-Wide Web?,” mycologist Dr. Justine Karst and colleagues evaluated dozens of CMN studies. They write that CMNs “have captured the interest of broad audiences. We are concerned, however, that recent claims about CMNs in forests are disconnected from evidence.” The student wants to use a quotation to emphasize a potential problem with the wood-wide web theory.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"The popular wood-wide web theory posits that trees can communicate and exchange resources with one another via common","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"mycorrhizal networks (CMNs) of fungi.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Ecologist Dr. Suzanne Simard first suggested this theory in 1997.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"She described trees as “super-cooperators.”","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"In the 2022 study “The Decay of the Wood-Wide Web?,” mycologist Dr. Justine Karst and colleagues evaluated dozens of CMN studies.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"They write that CMNs “have captured the interest of broad audiences. We are concerned, however, that recent claims about CMNs in","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"forests are disconnected from evidence.”","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Describing trees as “super-cooperators,” Simard first suggested that trees can exchange resources with one another in 1997.","In “The Decay of the Wood-Wide Web?,” Karst and colleagues note that common mycorrhizal networks “have captured the interest of broad audiences.”","After evaluating dozens of CMN studies, Karst and colleagues expressed concern that recent claims about common mycorrhizal networks are “disconnected from evidence.”","Despite the concerns expressed in the 2022 study “The Decay of the Wood-Wide Web?,” the wood-wide web theory remains popular."]}];
